<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yam (postal system)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yam was the relay and postal system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, a network of stations supplying fresh horses, food and lodging to official messengers and travellers. It allowed information and orders to travel rapidly across the vast empire. Accounts of the Mongol Empire treat the Yam as vital to both military coordination and imperial governance.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The yam, also called the örtöö, was a Mongol postal and supply-point system. Its name goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> period, when the word for &quot;post station staff&quot; in the Tuoba language was related to the Mongolian jamuchin. Nomadic and semi-nomadic peoples had long used postal systems, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and Jin dynasties maintained posts in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, so the Mongol yam is believed to have originated in the systems of the Eurasian steppe and northern China. According to The Secret History of the Mongols the yam was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a>, but other sources show that a rudimentary postal system already existed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> expanded into northern China they incorporated the existing Jin posts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a> gave the yam special attention. Mongol armies travelled quickly, so their messengers had to be faster still, and they covered 200 to 300 kilometres a day. Relay stations stood usually 20 to 40 miles apart. A messenger arrived at a station, passed his information to another messenger, and rested while the other rode on to the next station, so that information was constantly in motion. Each station kept spare horses, food and shelter. The system speeded up the flow of information and intelligence.</p><p>European travellers including Giovanni da Pian del Carpine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a> described the service in detail. Earlier messenger systems had existed in the Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empires</a>, but the yam was unprecedented in size and efficiency. As it expanded, Mongol war routes became commercial routes, and people and messages could travel from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> or from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> by horse or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> caravan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> replaced the old system of tax collection. Post roads with fixed stations were built after 1300, when the Mongols stopped keeping resident agents, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darughachi">basqaq</a>, and sent envoys whenever tribute was due. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> disintegrated in the 15th century, Russia kept the system for fast government communication and later for the postal service, as the yam courier service. It was levied as yam duty on both urban and rural populations and controlled by a yamskoy prikaz, and its coachmen were called yamshchiki. Many Russian cities had suburbs settled by them, called yamskaya sloboda, and places such as Yam-Tyosovo and Gavrilov-Yam keep the word in their names.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yam (route)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
